---
import ProjectLayout  from '@/layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
import ProjectSection from '@/components/ProjectSection.astro';
import ProjectGallery from '@/components/ProjectGallery.astro';
import SpecTable      from '@/components/SpecTable.astro';
import { getProject, getNeighbours } from '@/data/projects';

const p = getProject('cnc-lathe-conversion');
---

<ProjectLayout
  title={p.title}
  subtitle="Shop & Machine Projects · In progress"
  role={p.role}
  year={p.year}
  tags={p.tags}
  overview={p.summary}
  ogImage={p.cover}
  {...getNeighbours(p.slug)}
>

  <ProjectSection num="01" title="Why a through-bore" dark>
    <p>
      The augers the <a href="/research/gen-2-filament-research">second-generation filament machine</a>
      needs are longer than any machine I could fit in a home shop. A hollow
      rotary axis solves that. Instead of holding the whole screw inside the
      machine's travel, the bar <strong>passes through</strong> the rotary axis:
      an auger up to <strong>2 m</strong> long feeds through the bore and gets
      machined in roughly <strong>30 cm</strong> sections, indexing forward
      between them.
    </p>
    <p>
      The machine never has to be bigger than one section. That single decision
      is what makes precision augers possible at home rather than at a shop
      with a 2-meter lathe.
    </p>
  </ProjectSection>

  <ProjectSection num="02" title="The two conversions">
    <h3>South Bend lathe → CNC</h3>
    <SpecTable
      rows={[
        { key: 'Motion',     value: 'Stepper motors on both axes' },
        { key: 'Spindle',    value: 'VFD spindle control' },
        { key: 'Drivetrain', value: 'Modified leadscrews' },
        { key: 'Mounting',   value: 'Custom motor mounts designed for this machine' },
      ]}
    />

    <h3>Bridgeport mill → hollow-bore 4th axis</h3>
    <SpecTable
      rows={[
        { key: 'Added axis', value: 'Hollow-bore rotary axis' },
        { key: 'Control',    value: 'Own driver, added to the existing 3-axis control' },
        { key: 'Capacity',   value: 'Stock up to about 2 m fed through the bore, machined in ~30 cm sections' },
      ]}
    />
  </ProjectSection>

  <ProjectSection num="03" title="Safety and commissioning" dark>
    <p>
      Converting a manual machine to computer control removes the operator from
      the loop that used to make it safe. Before either machine cuts under
      program control, I wrote a full risk assessment and approval package
      against:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>ISO 12100</strong> — risk assessment and risk reduction</li>
      <li><strong>ANSI B11</strong> — machine tool safety requirements</li>
      <li><strong>NFPA 79</strong> — electrical standard for industrial machinery</li>
      <li><strong>OSHA lockout/tagout</strong> — energy isolation during service</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The package includes a witnessed commissioning plan, so first powered
      motion happens with a second person present and a defined abort.
    </p>
  </ProjectSection>

  <ProjectSection num="04" title="Training">
    <SpecTable
      caption="Training in progress — none of these are complete yet"
      rows={[
        { key: 'SolidWorks',      value: 'Working toward CSWA — Certified SolidWorks Associate' },
        { key: 'Autodesk Fusion', value: 'Working toward the CAM certification — mill and turn' },
        { key: 'Titans of CNC',   value: 'Working through the machining curriculum' },
      ]}
    />

    <ProjectGallery
      cols={2}
      images={[
        {
          src: '/images/workshop.jpg',
          alt: 'Benjamin Davis cutting steel stock with an angle grinder in his home shop, wearing a respirator and ear defenders',
          caption: 'Stock prep in the home shop.',
        },
        {
          alt: 'The South Bend lathe and Bridgeport mill installed in the home shop, before conversion',
          pending: 'Photo to come — lathe and mill as delivered',
          caption: 'Both machines are in the shop; conversion photos follow as the work progresses.',
        },
      ]}
    />
  </ProjectSection>

  <ProjectSection num="05" title="Credits" dark>
    <p>
      A local network of machinists and neighbours helped me find, move and
      install both machines — neither a lathe nor a Bridgeport arrives in a
      home shop without a lot of people lending trailers, engine hoists and
      advice.
    </p>
  </ProjectSection>

</ProjectLayout>
